1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 b6 5. Qg4 Bf8 6. Bg5 Qd7 7. O-O-O h6 8. Bh4
$4 g5 9. Bg3 (9. Bxg5 hxg5 10. Kb1 Be7 11. Nf3 Nh6 12. Qh5 g4 13. Ng5 Ba6 14.
Bxa6 Nxa6 15. h3 gxh3 16. Rxh3 O-O-O 17. Rdh1 Rhg8 18. Nf3 Ng4 19. Rf1 Rdf8 20.
Nh2 Nxh2 21. Rxh2 c5 22. Qe2 Nb4 23. dxc5 Bxc5 24. g4 d4 25. Qf3 Kb8 26. Rd1
Qc6 27. Ne4 Qd5 28. b3 Qxe5 29. Rh7 Nd5 30. Nxc5 bxc5 31. Rdh1 Rg7 32. Rxg7
Qxg7 33. Qg3+ Ka8 34. Qd6 Rc8 35. Qd7 Nb6 36. Qe7 d3 37. cxd3 Na4 38. bxa4 Qc3
{was the continuation in Padevsky-Portisch, Varna Olympiad 1962. Keres had
played in this event but forgotten about this trap - Barden, CHESS}) 9... h5
10. Qf3 h4 {"and wins the B for a pawn" but "Keres wriggled so ingeniously
that he managed to salvage a draw" Source: CHESS, Vol.28 No.422, 20 December
1962} 1/2-1/2

